/*!
    \page ascent-rounds.html
    \title The Round State Machine
    \previouspage Provably Fair Randomness
    \nextpage Juice

    A crash game has very few moves, and almost all of the work is refusing them
    at the wrong moment. This chapter is about which moves are legal when.

    \section1 The Four States of a Round

    \snippet src/RoundEngine.h round-states

    Betting takes stakes and nothing else. Running climbs and accepts exactly one
    cash out. Crashed is the moment the balloon pops, and Settled is the few
    seconds of results before the next round opens.

    Every public method on the engine starts by checking the state, and returns
    \c false rather than throwing or asserting. The caller is a user interface,
    and a refused tap is a normal thing for a user interface to do.

    The engine is also told where the round pops instead of deciding it - the
    commitment from the previous chapter was published before any of this ran,
    and an engine that could pick its own crash point would quietly undo that.

    \section1 Placing a Bet

    A bet is only accepted while betting is open and only once per round. The
    second condition matters more than it looks: without it a player could add
    to their stake at 3x, which is not betting, it is buying a result.

    The stake leaves the wallet immediately. Points that are in play are gone
    until a cash out puts them back, so there is no way to bet the same points
    twice by acting quickly.

    \section1 Cashing Out Before the Pop

    \snippet src/RoundEngine.cpp cash-out

    Three refusals in one condition, and each is a bug somebody has shipped: not
    while Running means no cashing out after the pop, \c m_hasCashedOut means no
    collecting twice on one stake, and the bet check means no payout for having
    watched a round.

    \image ascent-cashed-out.png Cashed out at 1.40x while the round is still going. The number keeps climbing and the player no longer has anything riding on it - which is most of what makes the game hurt

    The payout uses \c m_multiplier - the value the player can read - and not the
    curve behind it. The difference is fractions of a percent per round, and it
    is the difference between a game that pays what it displays and one that
    argues about rounding.

    That value is set in exactly one place:

    \snippet src/RoundEngine.cpp advance-to

    The engine has no clock. Something outside advances it, which is why a test
    can run a full round in microseconds while the game runs the same code off a
    16 ms timer. Truncation to two decimals happens here and nowhere else, and on
    the crash the multiplier is pinned to the committed point rather than to
    whatever the curve had reached by the time the timer last fired.

    \section1 Handling the Felgo Splash Screen

    With a free license Felgo shows a splash screen for the first few seconds.
    The game behind it is already running, which for a game with a permanent
    round cycle means the first round starts while the player is still looking at
    a logo. They arrive to a round they never had a chance to bet on.

    \snippet qml/AscentMain.qml splash

    One signal instead of \c Component.onCompleted. Worth knowing before you
    build anything that starts moving on its own.

    \section1 Where C++ Ends and QML Begins

    The line is drawn at "what is true" versus "what it looks like".

    C++ owns the seed, the crash point, the state machine, the balance, and the
    clock. QML owns the balloon, the colours, the countdown text, and which
    button is offered. When the interface wants something to happen it asks:
    \c {Rounds.requestBet()} and \c {Rounds.requestCashOut()} return nothing, and
    the answer arrives as a signal.

    That indirection looks like ceremony in a single-player game where the answer
    is instant. It is there because the same screen has to work when the answer
    comes from a server instead - which is the subject of the last chapter.

    \include includes/chapters.qdocinc chapters
*/
